So the next one came from Cope. So Cope was known for being an incredibly fast writer, and he would just fire off paper after paper after paper. And Dan Brinkman at the Peabody Museum told me about a mistake he made that was more of a clerical error than a taxonomic error as he was rushing to beat Marsh to publication. Cope had kind of poor handwriting that alone when he's writing by a lamplight at Cam. But a poor telegrapher in Blackbeaut, Wyoming got this such line description that he was to telegraph back to Philadelphia. Cope had intended to name one of these animals, Loxalafidon, which means beast with crested tooth. But the poor telegrapher, trying to make it out, bastardized it into Leffalafidon, which is pure gibberish. Well, that's happened to all of us, I think.
接下来的一个错误来自科普。科普以写作速度极快而闻名，他会接连不断地发表论文。皮博迪博物馆的丹·布林克曼（Dan Brinkman）告诉我，他犯了一个错误，与其说是一个分类学错误，不如说是一个文书错误，因为他当时正赶着抢在马什之前发表。科普的字迹本来就有点糟糕，当他在凌晨的煤油灯下写作时更是如此。但在怀俄明州布莱克福特（Black Butte）的一个糟糕的电报员收到了这样一行描述，他需要把这行描述电报回费城。科普原本打算给其中一种动物命名为 Loxalafidon，意思是“长着冠状牙齿的野兽”。但那个倒霉的电报员为了辨认清楚，把它曲解成了 Leffalafidon，这纯粹是胡言乱语。好吧，我想我们所有人都遇到过这种情况。

